                <pb facs="0789" n="690" sameAs="frus:frus1936v05/pg690"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">815.00 Revolutions/511: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Honduras</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Keena</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-04-15T15:00:00-06:00">April 15, 1936—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:03 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>41. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 22, April 14, 7 p.m. The suggestion I
                    made in telegram No. 39, April 11, noon, concerning a naval vessel cruising in
                    the vicinity of Honduras was based on the belief that the moral effect would be
                    good in the event of an outbreak of hostilities on the north coast. The
                    suggestion was not prompted by nor communicated to either the Honduran
                    authorities or any American citizen.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Keena</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
